{"outcome": {"quote": "Our ﬁndings were mostly consistent with those of the original study (see details below), except that we did not ﬁnd that gender moderated the
indirect effects of company diversity on interest in working for the company via the perception of the company’s sincere interest in increasing

gender diversity.", "location": "Summary of Study and Results (p. 4)", "reason": "The authors explicitly frame their findings as only partly supporting the original study (13 of 17 key findings replicated), contrasting their null result for gender moderation of the indirect effect on company interest—one of the two main conditional process models tested—and the lack of difference between counterfeit diversity and authentic non-diversity with the original Study 3 findings.", "method": "argon"}}

{"outcome": {"quote": "The second finding (
Table II
) partially replicated our previous work
33
revealing statistically significant pre- to post-intervention improvements in affect, anxiety, and craving. However, this was only true for the first two or three of the possible four sessions.", "location": "discussion", "reason": "The shipped quote covers only safety and feasibility, whereas the authors explicitly state in the discussion that the psychological outcome findings only partially replicated their previous study because significant improvements were limited to the first two or three of the four sessions. Because the authors themselves frame their results as partially replicating the original work, the outcome is mixed.", "method": "argon"}}

{"type": {"quote": "The study’s initial population of more than 300 million tweet IDs processed in a data collection pipeline is not publicly available. However, the replication archive allows access to all tweet IDs classified by the article’s deep-learning algorithm as containing violent content.
Footnote
12
This set of IDs ranges from September 23, 2020 to January 8, 2021 and consists of 215,923 unique tweet IDs. As of November 15, 2022, there are only 35,552 (16.47%) of the original number of tweets retrievable via the API.", "location": "Section 4 (Case Study: Implications for Replicating Sensitive Twitter Studies), lines 205-208", "reason": "Although the author uses the term 'replication', the study rehydrates the original 215,923 tweet IDs and auxiliary data from Kim's (2023) replication archive to re-run the original analyses on the surviving subset of original observations rather than collecting a new sample, making it a reproduction under the codebook.", "method": "argon"}, "outcome": {"quote": "Comparing the original model 5 with regressions using the recrawled dataset (see Figure
6
) reveals that one of the paper’s key findings of having more women targeted by violent tweets does not hold anymore. The estimate of the effect is close to zero, and the coefficient’s 95% confidence interval of the recrawled model widely includes zero, too.", "location": "Section 4.2 (Replication: Statistical Model Findings), lines 287-289", "reason": "Re-running the original descriptive and regression analyses on the recrawled archive data produces comparable numerical output that differs considerably from the published figures, and nonrandom tweet attrition removes the key finding that women are disproportionately targeted by violent tweets.", "method": "argon"}}

{"type": {"quote": "In the original paper the estimate is positive (0.84) and significant. In the reproduction, the estimated coefficient was positive (0.84) and significant (p=0.000).

Thus the reproduction claim was successful according to the SCORE criteria.", "location": "Replication Results > Reproduction", "reason": "This hybrid SCORE report conducts both an extended-data replication and a same-data reproduction using Powell's original dataset, so coding this row as a reproduction is directly supported by the source.", "method": "argon"}, "outcome_computation": {"quote": "In the original paper the estimate is positive (0.84) and significant. In the reproduction, the estimated coefficient was positive (0.84) and significant (p=0.000).

Thus the reproduction claim was successful according to the SCORE criteria.", "location": "Replication Results > Reproduction", "reason": "Both the shipped row and the blind coder agree that re-running the focal model on the original dataset reproduced the original estimate of 0.84 (p=0.000).", "method": "argon"}, "outcome_robustness": {"quote": "There are no deviations for the reproduction data", "location": "Deviations from the Original Study", "reason": "Both the shipped row and the blind coder agree that no alternative specifications or robustness checks were conducted on the reproduction dataset.", "method": "argon"}}

{"outcome_robustness": {"quote": "The claim selected for the original study yielded a negative regression interaction term in the focal test, corresponding to an increased local degree proportion reducing the effect of holding a four-year degree on the use of verbal skills. The replication yielded a positive regression interaction term in the focal test, corresponding to an effect in a different pattern compared to the original study.

Deviations from preregistration.
There were no deviations from the preregistration plan, or changes to the preregistered script (except to remove the line limiting analysis to a random 5% subset of the data).", "location": "Replication Result / Deviations from preregistration, lines 852-860", "reason": "The report conducts only a single preregistered reproduction attempt (Model 1) reconstructing the original analysis from the paper's description and raw data sources, which fails to recover the original negative interaction coefficient (coded on Axis 1 as 'computational issues'). The differences in sample restriction, occupation matching, and CFA construction are documented as unavoidable reconstruction deviations due to omitted details in the original paper and code rather than alternative specifications or sensitivity analyses tested by the reproducer. Because no robustness checks or alternative specifications were conducted, outcome_robustness is 'not checked'.", "method": "argon"}}

{"outcome": {"quote": "Based on the above, we summarize this replication as partly successful. We discuss

potential reasons for the discrepancies between the original results and ours and their implications

in the general discussion.", "location": "Study 2 Discussion, p. 48 (lines 1810-1814)", "reason": "In Study 2 (replicating Experiment 1 of Connolly et al., 2013), the authors replicated the baseline decoy effect and the choice justifiability finding, found inconclusive/null results for regret salience and choice regret, and explicitly gave an overall verdict summarizing the replication as 'partly successful' (mixed). The shipped quote describes the low-reversibility extension rather than an uninformative verdict on the replication.", "method": "argon"}}

{"outcome": {"quote": "This investigation replicates and extends an investigation by Wei et al. (
2024
) regarding the effects of prompts-activated purposeful reading, pre-reading expert referent networks (Study 1), and post-reading network-based writing feedback (Study 2) on the quantity and quality of multiple document integration. As expected and consistent with Wei et al. (
2024
), pre-reading integrative prompts improved the quantity of integration in both studies (i.e., η
2
values are around 0.50).", "location": "General discussion, lines 995-1000", "reason": "The replicated component from Wei et al. (2024) is the manipulation of pre-reading prompts (integrative vs. detailed prompts), for which Wei et al. (2024) found a positive effect on integration quantity but not on semantic or structural quality (lines 307-311). Both Study 1 and Study 2 replicate this exact pattern (significant prompt effect on integration quantity and no main effect of prompts on semantic or structural quality; lines 607-703, 870-930, 1031-1032), and the authors explicitly conclude that their investigation agrees with and supports Wei et al. (2024) (Abstract, lines 55-56; General discussion, lines 995-1000; Conclusion, lines 1107-1109). The difference in delayed-writing structural quality (GC_sim) noted in lines 1050-1066 is explicitly attributed by the authors to the new extension intervention—giving all participants an expert referent network before reading or as post-writing feedback, which was not present in Wei et al. (2024) ('Note that participants were not given an expert network in that investigation. This suggests that the expert network, whether given before reading or as writing feedback, influences the conceptual structure of delayed essays')—rather than a failed replication of an original claim.", "method": "argon"}}
